WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:11.603
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with people from

2
00:00:11.604 --> 00:00:20.077
the past. I'm Faye Moss. Today's guest never forgot her humble background when she became world

3
00:00:20.078 --> 00:00:27.154
famous. As the First Lady of Argentina, she worked tirelessly to help the working class.

4
00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:46.002
Straight from history, here is Eva Perón!

5
00:00:46.003 --> 00:00:52.881
EVA PERÓN: Thank you, Faye. Please—call me Evita. To my people I was always

6
00:00:52.882 --> 00:01:02.284
Evita. FAYE MOSS: Okay, Evita! You were born in May 1919 in the small village of Los

7
00:01:02.285 --> 00:01:05.049
Toldos, Argentina. What was your

8
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.770
childhood like? EVA PERÓN: I was the youngest of five children, and my mother

9
00:01:09.771 --> 00:01:13.226
raised us alone. As a child I was aware that

10
00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:19.400
we weren't rich. Argentina was a prosperous country, but privileged landowners had all

11
00:01:19.401 --> 00:01:25.264
the wealth. One time I heard a working man say that the rich were too

12
00:01:25.265 --> 00:01:27.994
rich . I never

13
00:01:27.995 --> 00:01:31.865
forgot that! FAYE MOSS: Did you

14
00:01:31.866 --> 00:01:35.501
dream of becoming famous? EVA

15
00:01:35.502 --> 00:01:43.948
PERÓN: Yes, but not through politics! After appearing in a school play, I dreamed of becoming an actor in Buenos Aires, which was

16
00:01:43.949 --> 00:01:49.030
a huge, glamorous city. At 15 I moved to Buenos

17
00:01:49.031 --> 00:01:53.539
Aires and began working. I got small parts on stage, in film,

18
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.716
and on radio. Soon I became a radio

19
00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:02.023
soap opera star. My real life began when I

20
00:02:02.024 --> 00:02:08.348
met Perón. FAYE MOSS: You're referring to Colonel Juan Perón!

21
00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:16.619
How did you meet? EVA PERÓN: In January 1944 an earthquake struck the city of San Juan, and

22
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:22.417
six thousand people were killed. Perón planned a huge gala to collect donations

23
00:02:22.418 --> 00:02:28.236
for the earthquake victims. We met there, and from then on, I was

24
00:02:28.237 --> 00:02:36.329
always by his side. FAYE MOSS: Colonel Perón was a high-profile member of the military government that was

25
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:44.099
ruling Argentina at the time. EVA PERÓN: Yes. As secretary of labor, Perón gave workers a minimum

26
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.635
wage, paid holidays, and medical care. Never before had an Argentine leader

27
00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:54.795
cared so much about workers! Several months after we first met,

28
00:02:54.796 --> 00:03:03.397
he was appointed vice president. Unfortunately his rapid rise created enemies, and many within the

29
00:03:03.398 --> 00:03:09.057
government resented Perón's popularity. FAYE MOSS: Tell us about the

30
00:03:09.058 --> 00:03:14.363
turbulent events of October 1945. EVA PERÓN: The factions against Perón

31
00:03:14.364 --> 00:03:22.544
pressured him to resign, and he agreed. But in a farewell speech, he announced wage increases

32
00:03:22.545 --> 00:03:25.615
for workers, which infuriated his enemies. A few

33
00:03:25.616 --> 00:03:35.461
days later, Perón was arrested. On October 17 huge crowds of workers peacefully marched to Casa Rosada, the seat of the government,

34
00:03:35.462 --> 00:03:43.248
to protest the arrest. FAYE MOSS: They were called the descamisados —the shirtless ones.

35
00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:49.252
How did they get that name, Evita? EVA PERÓN: Many marchers took off their coats, and some

36
00:03:49.253 --> 00:03:55.171
even their shirts, because the weather was hot! With over 200,000 supporters in the

37
00:03:55.172 --> 00:04:00.003
public square, Perón was released from jail. The people cheered for 15 minutes

38
00:04:00.004 --> 00:04:03.907
when he stepped onto the balcony! FAYE MOSS:

39
00:04:03.908 --> 00:04:12.742
You and Perón were married the next day. When the military government agreed to disband and hold a free election,

40
00:04:12.743 --> 00:04:17.002
Juan Perón decided to run for president. You

41
00:04:17.003 --> 00:04:24.724
were highly visible during his campaign. EVA PERÓN: Many disapproved of that! In Latin America,

42
00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:31.877
wives stayed at home and out of politics. But Perón and I were a team, and this continued

43
00:04:31.878 --> 00:04:35.775
after he was elected in February 1946. As first

44
00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:40.216
lady I met with the descamisados . When giving speeches

45
00:04:40.217 --> 00:04:45.985
I always spoke passionately, from my heart. They would shout,

46
00:04:45.986 --> 00:04:49.732
"Perón! Evita! Our lives for Perón!" I was also

47
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:57.970
vocal about equal rights for women. I organized and became president of the Peronista Women's Party and supported a bill

48
00:04:57.971 --> 00:05:04.289
that allowed women to vote. FAYE MOSS: The Argentine workers loved you and Perón,

49
00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.202
but you also had

50
00:05:07.203 --> 00:05:11.710
your enemies. EVA PERÓN: The descamisados loved us. When they asked for 40 percent

51
00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:17.187
higher wages, we gave them 50 percent! We did a lot of good for them—shortening their

52
00:05:17.188 --> 00:05:24.161
work hours and building them houses. But the wealthy, the students, the intellectuals . . . they hated

53
00:05:24.162 --> 00:05:34.140
us and called us fascists! This didn't deter me. I owned two newspapers and controlled several radio stations in Buenos Aires, so I suppressed criticism

54
00:05:34.141 --> 00:05:41.110
of Perón and me. Yes, we silenced our critics at home, but we did so to

55
00:05:41.111 --> 00:05:45.985
protect our descamisados . Unfortunately I couldn't stop the critics

56
00:05:45.986 --> 00:05:51.092
when I visited Europe. FAYE MOSS: You're talking about

57
00:05:51.093 --> 00:05:59.296
your Rainbow Tour in 1947. You were the biggest news story all over the world! The media

58
00:05:59.297 --> 00:06:06.497
followed your every move. EVA PERÓN: Yes, I wanted to be a rainbow—a bridge between

59
00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:14.179
Argentina and the countries I visited. I had planned to visit Britain, but the press called me the wife of a dictator! I

60
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:21.772
canceled my visit there. In Spain the crowds greeted me so warmly! And in Italy

61
00:06:21.773 --> 00:06:29.043
I met the Pope. I also visited France and Switzerland. After two months I was eager to return to my

62
00:06:29.044 --> 00:06:34.003
three great loves: Argentina, my descamisados

63
00:06:34.004 --> 00:06:40.991
, and Perón. FAYE MOSS: When you returned to Argentina, you established the Eva Perón

64
00:06:40.992 --> 00:06:48.733
Foundation, a charity that— EVA PERÓN: No, Faye! This was not a

65
00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:54.778
charity. My foundation provided social justice. We gave the poor what we owed them so there would be

66
00:06:54.779 --> 00:07:00.404
fewer rich and fewer poor! And the Argentine people were so

67
00:07:00.405 --> 00:07:09.963
generous; they gave voluntarily. Well . . . some had to be pressured to contribute. We built schools, hospitals, parks,

68
00:07:09.964 --> 00:07:14.082
and recreation centers. Women and children lined up at my office to tell

69
00:07:14.083 --> 00:07:20.121
me their needs. I made sure that they received clothing, furniture, medicine, toys,

70
00:07:20.122 --> 00:07:26.168
and cash. They came to me defeated. I understood their suffering and

71
00:07:26.169 --> 00:07:32.569
injustice. That was why, in my speeches, I often shouted that justice would be

72
00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:36.715
done! FAYE MOSS: Your work earned you the nickname The Lady

73
00:07:36.716 --> 00:07:46.881
of Hope. You were so popular that when Perón ran for reelection in 1951, the people wanted you to be vice

74
00:07:46.882 --> 00:07:51.739
president! No woman had ever run for such a powerful position.

75
00:07:51.740 --> 00:08:01.363
EVA PERÓN: When Perón announced his candidacy, the huge crowd chanted, "Evita! Evita!" hoping I would join the

76
00:08:01.364 --> 00:08:09.287
ticket. I said no, but the crowd protested. So I asked for four days, then two, then one day to decide.

77
00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:13.180
Still they shouted, "Evita! Evita!"

78
00:08:13.181 --> 00:08:20.316
As I had not been feeling well for some time, I officially declined over a week later. Of

79
00:08:20.317 --> 00:08:28.483
course, Perón was reelected! Millions of women, including me, cast our first votes. FAYE MOSS:

80
00:08:28.484 --> 00:08:36.034
Evita, thanks so much for joining us! We'll let you get back to history now.

81
00:08:36.035 --> 00:08:40.266
EVA PERÓN: Thank you,

82
00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:46.670
Faye. FAYE MOSS: Unknown to the public, Evita's health had been declining due

83
00:08:46.671 --> 00:08:55.222
to advanced cancer. Shortly into Juan Perón's second term, Eva Perón died on July

84
00:08:55.223 --> 00:09:05.280
26, 1952. She was 33 years old. Flowers sold out in Buenos Aires as Argentina plunged into

85
00:09:05.281 --> 00:09:11.823
mourning. Over two million mourners came to see their beloved Evita lying in state.

86
00:09:11.824 --> 00:09:21.521
In an era when women had little role in politics, Evita was known around the world for her political activities. Her

87
00:09:21.522 --> 00:09:28.140
story eventually became immortalized in the rock opera Evita .

88
00:09:28.141 --> 00:09:36.915
I hope you enjoyed today's show. See you next time on Superstars in History .

